ebra star-squares.\n    53\t  An explicit `StarAlgEquiv` transports this finite type-I product trace to a\n    54\t  target algebra.\n    55\t- `TMT-001` — the finite type-I product is now identified canonically, not\n    56\t  supplied as an assumption: `Matrix n n ℂ ⊗[ℂ] R` is star-isomorphic to\n    57\t  `Matrix n n R`.  Its trace factors on elementary tensors as the normalized\n    58\t  matrix trace times the coefficient trace.\n    59\t- `DTT-001` — the actual dyadic finite-stage system is constructed as\n    60\t  `A₀ = ℂ`, `Aₙ₊₁ = Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) Aₙ`, with diagonal identity-qubit\n    61\t  embeddings.  Its normalized traces are compatible with every stage map,\n    62\t  and their algebraic directed limit carries the induced algebraic tracial\n    63\t  state.\n    64\t- FPU-001 — for every nonempty finite matrix factor, its algebraic tensor\n    65\t  product with the concrete dyadic algebraic limit carries the checked product\n    66\t  tracial state.  The theorem includes the elementary-tensor formula\n    67\t  tr_p(a) times qubitUHFTrace(u).\n    68\t- FPU-002 — any supplied regional `StarAlgEquiv` into that finite-pure\n    69\t  product model induces an algebraic tracial state on the regional algebra,\n    70\t  with the checked formula on inverse images of elementary tensors.\n    71\t- `AIT-001` — compatible unital cyclic finite-stage functionals glue to a\n    72\t  unital cyclic linear functional on mathlib's algebraic directed direct\n    73\t  limit.\n    74\t- `AIT-002` — compatible stagewise star-algebra maps induce an involutive,\n    75\t  additive, multiplication-reversing star on that algebraic direct limit.\n    76\t  Compatible algebraic tracial states then glue to an ordinary algebraic\n    77\t  tracial state there.\n    78\t- `SPC-001` — an adapted Hamel basis identifies the label space with finitely\n    79\t  supported X/Z coordinate labels and maps the singleton coordinate labels to\n    80\t  the chosen basis vectors.\n    81\t- ASC-001 — for a selected set of Z coordinates, Lean now identifies the\n    82\t  corresponding coordinate subspace both as the span of those Z basis\n    83\t  vectors and as the finite words with no X coordinate and no Z coordinate\n    84\t  outside the selected set.  This is the exact linear selection criterion\n    85\t  required before comparing the regional stabilizer state with the\n    86\t  pure/mixed product state.\n    87\t- FQS-001 — Lean now reads the recursive finite-qubit Pauli label as its\n    88\t  finite X/Z coordinate list and proves that the pure/mixed product-state\n    89\t  indicator is exactly the selected-Z condition: a pure coordinate has no X\n    90\t  exponent, and a mixed coordinate is the identity label.  Under an arbitrary\n    91\t  supplied adapted coordinate basis, that condition is literal membership in\n    92\t  the selected-Z stabilizer submodule.  The coherent direct-limit state theorem\n    93\t  has a corresponding selected-Z indicator form on source basis words.\n    94\t- `PCC-001` — a phase-corrected additive map of Pauli labels respects word\n    95\t  multiplication and star exactly when it satisfies explicit multiplier and\n    96\t  involution equations.  This isolates the cocycle data that symplecticity\n    97\t  alone does not supply.  The cross-algebra word-basis extension is now also\n    98\t  checked: its two compatibility equations and a specified Pauli-word basis\n    99\t  yield a genuine `StarAlgEquiv`.\n   100\t- `SCC-001` — the quotient of two multiplier phases is an additive character,\n   101\t  calibrating by an additive character preserves the multiplier equation, and\n   102\t  every additive character on a vector subspace extends to the ambient vector\n   103\t  space along a complementary subspace.  This is the exact algebraic\n   104\t  stabilizer-sign calibration: the finite-field character is self-conjugate,\n   105\t  and calibration preserves both Pauli-word compatibility equations.  The\n   106\t  concrete finite-support quadratic phase is now independently formalized;\n   107\t  relating it to the actual regional stabilizer convention remains separate.\n   108\t- QPR-001 — the explicit ZMod 4 finite-support quadratic refinement has its\n   109\t  polarization and diagonal identities fully checked.  Its discrepancy pairing\n   110\t  is additive in both words, symmetric for a symmetric discrepancy, and has\n   111\t  additive order two.  These facts instantiate the cross-algebra Pauli-word\n   112\t  multiplication and involution certificates and, with a supplied word-basis\n   113\t  linear equivalence, a `StarAlgEquiv`.\n   114\t- QPT-001 — the concrete finite-support phase theorem now composes that\n   115\t  `StarAlgEquiv` with the finite-pure times qubit-UHF trace transport: whenever\n   116\t  the regional Pauli-word exponents, involution phases, and word-basis map are\n   117\t  supplied, Lean constructs both the star-algebra equivalence and its induced\n   118\t  algebraic tracial state with the elementary-tensor product formula.  The\n   119\t  Pauli-word interface has been weakened from `Ring` to `Semiring`, the exact\n   120\t  structure carried by the concrete algebraic UHF direct limit.\n   121\t- PTO-001 — two isometries whose range projections sum to the identity rule\n   122\t  out even an algebraic unital cyclic trace.  This is the checked obstruction\n   123\t  behind the infinite-pure-sector case; the tracial-state theorem is therefore\n   124\t  deliberately limited to a finite pure matrix factor.\n   125\t- ALT-001 — compatible finite-stage `StarAlgEquiv`s and compatible target\n   126\t  algebraic tracial states induce a source-side algebraic tracial state on the\n   127\t  corresponding quasi-local direct limit.  The theorem evaluates it exactly\n   128\t  on every finite-stage representative.  The concrete finite-pure UHF trace\n   129\t  now has the matching finite-stage elementary-tensor formula.\n   130\t- FQP-001 — every finite stage of the dyadic qubit tower now has an explicit\n   131\t  ordered Pauli-word `StarSystem` and a complex Pauli-word basis.  The\n   132\t  identity-qubit embeddings preserve its words, basis vectors, multiplication\n   133\t  multipliers, and star multipliers under coherent label maps.  Thus the UHF\n   134\t  target-side finite-stage word hypotheses are no longer abstract assumptions;\n   135\t  assembling them into one global target word system remains open.\n   136\t- PMS-001 — for every Boolean sequence specifying whether each newly adjoined\n   137\t  qubit is pure or maximally mixed, the corresponding finite product\n   138\t  functionals are algebraic states, are compatible with all dyadic embeddings,\n   139\t  and induce an algebraic state on the UHF direct limit.  Their values on every\n   140\t  finite Pauli word are proved recursively: pure coordinates permit only zero\n   141\t  X exponent, while mixed coordinates permit only the identity word.  The\n   142\t  all-mixed specialization is proved equal to the previously checked UHF\n   143\t  tracial state.  Lean also proves that even the one-qubit pure specialization\n   144\t  is not cyclic and hence not an algebraic tracial state.\n   145\t- PTR-001 — this nontraciality is now proved at arbitrary finite and\n   146\t  direct-limit coordinates: if any Boolean coordinate is pure, explicit\n   147\t  matrix units witness a failure of cyclicity.  Consequently the algebraic\n   148\t  UHF product state is tracial if and only if every coordinate is maximally\n   149\t  mixed, in which case it is the checked UHF trace.\n   150\t- CBL-001 — finite-stage star-algebra equivalences need not be assumed\n   151\t  coherent on every algebra element: if the source connecting maps, target\n   152\t  vectors, and coordinate maps agree on a specified source basis, Lean proves\n   153\t  the full commuting square by linear-basis extensionality.\n   154\t- CPS-001 — a phase-corrected finite coordinate system now has a concrete\n   155\t  direct-limit state interface.  Coherent source basis transport, phases, and\n   156\t  finite Pauli labels imply full-stage equivalence coherence and hence a\n   157\t  star-algebra equivalence to the algebraic qubit-UHF direct limit; the induced\n   158\t  source state evaluates a basis word as its phase times the pure/mixed Pauli\n   159\t  selection indicator.  The all-mixed specialization pulls the UHF trace back\n   160\t  to a source algebraic tracial state; more generally its pure/mixed pullback\n   161\t  is tracial exactly when every coordinate is mixed.  Constructing the\n   162\t  regional inputs remains open.\n   163\t- ACS-001 — if supplied finite-stage regional data identify selected target\n   164\t  Z-coordinates with the actual source stabilizer words, Lean derives the\n   165\t  exact phase-calibrated stabilizer expectation formula on every source basis word.\n   166\t  The stronger form states that hypothesis as literal membership in the\n   167\t  selected-Z submodule of an adapted coordinate basis.  Thus the remaining\n   168\t  geometric work is isolated to constructing and coherently transporting that\n   169\t  coordinate data from a separately supplied finite-coordinate realization,\n   170\t  rather than to a hidden infinite-algebra assumption. The manuscript does not\n   171\t  presently supply that realization; see `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`.\n   172\t- ALS-001 — compatible finite-stage star-algebra equivalences transport\n   173\t  compatible algebraic states (not only tracial states) to a quasi-local\n   174\t  algebraic direct limit.  This is the exact state-level interface required by\n   175\t  a coherent regional stabilizer-coordinate construction.\n   176\t- WBE-001 — if Pauli words form a complex linear basis, checking a\n   177\t  complex-linear map on every pair of basis words proves multiplicativity on\n   178\t  all finite sums.  The corresponding conjugate-linear basis argument proves\n   179\t  star preservation; a bijective unital map satisfying both checks is packaged\n   180\t  as a star-algebra equivalence.\n   181\t- ALC-001 — a compatible family of finite-stage star-algebra maps induces a\n   182\t  star-algebra map from the algebraic directed limit.  Compatible finite-stage\n   183\t  star-algebra equivalences induce a star-algebra equivalence of the two\n   184\t  algebraic limits.  The C-star norm extension remains open.\n   185\t- CSE-001 — once a source and target are supplied as complex C-star algebras,\n   186\t  Lean proves that every complex `StarAlgEquiv` is isometric and therefore\n   187\t  continuous with continuous inverse.  A continuous target functional pulls\n   188\t  back continuously along it.  This does not construct C-star norms or\n   189\t  completions for the algebraic direct-limit models.\n   190\t- CCF-001 — the pinned Mathlib supports the finite dyadic stages as genuine\n   191\t  C-star algebras and supports closure/minimality once an ambient C-star\n   192\t  algebra is supplied.  It does not provide the C-star inductive-limit\n   193\t  construction for the algebraic dyadic limit; this is recorded as an\n   194\t  explicit missing API rather than hidden behind an axiom.\n   195\t- NMC-001 — supplied finite C-star realizations of the coherent coordinate\n   196\t  maps are isometric and continuous, and the commuting inclusion squares\n   197\t  preserve the norm estimates.  The resulting dense-subalgebra estimate is\n   198\t  exactly the analytic input needed before extending the algebraic map to a\n   199\t  completion; no completion is constructed here.\n   200\t- BMC-001 — for a star subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\n   201\t  Lean now constructs the algebraic bicommutant as a concrete\n   202\t  `VonNeumannAlgebra`, with monotonicity, minimality, membership, and\n   203\t  idempotence lemmas.  The analytic strong/weak double-commutant theorem is\n   204\t  still explicitly absent from the pinned Mathlib and remains separate.\n   205\t- CCC-001 — from an explicitly supplied isometric star representation of a\n   206\t  pre-C-star algebra into bounded operators, Lean constructs the closed\n   207\t  operator-range C-star algebra, its isometric dense embedding, and the\n   208\t  uniqueness equivalence between two such concrete completions.  The faithful\n   209\t  operator representation of the project-specific algebraic limit is still a\n   210\t  separate obligation.\n   211\t\n   212\t## The exact theorem still to prove\n   213\t\n   214\tThe Overleaf short-range theorem asserts a regional von Neumann algebra\n   215\tisomorphism to a type-I factor tensor a hyperfinite type-II1 factor.  It is\n   216\tnot yet formalized as a theorem because the supplied manuscript proof leaves\n   217\tthe following essential premises open:\n   218\t\n   219\t1. a finite-support adapted symplectic coordinate map whose finite-stage\n   220\t   source bases, phase correction, and target Pauli labels are coherent along\n   221\t   an exhaustion;\n   222\t2. identification of the actual regional Pauli multiplier and involution\n   223\t   exponents with the finite-support coordinate discrepancy, followed by\n   224\t   calibration to the actual stabilizer character;\n   225\t3. the induced quasi-local C-star isomorphism;\n   226\t4. factorization of the regional state into pure and normalized-tracial\n   227\t   coordinate states; and\n   228\t5. a normal/spatial extension to the respective bicommutants.\n   229\t\n   230\tThe current pinned mathlib version includes preliminary abstract and concrete\n   231\tvon Neumann-algebra definitions, but lacks a finished API for infinite von\n   232\tNeumann tensor products, normal states/traces, hyperfiniteness, and factor\n   233\tclassification.  The project therefore does not introduce any axiom for the\n   234\ttarget theorem.\n   235\t\n   236\t## Last verification\n   237\t\n   238\t```text\n   239\tlake build\n   240\tlake build Formalization.AlgebraicLimitStateTransport Formalization.QubitMatrixState Formalization.FinitePureMixedQubitState Formalization\n   241\tlake build Formalization.TraceTransport Formalization\n   242\tlake build Formalization.CoherentBasisEquiv Formalization.CoherentPhaseQubitState Formalization\n   243\tlake build Formalization.PureCoordinateNotTracial Formalization\n   244\tlake build Formalization.AdaptedStabilizerSubspace Formalization\n   245\tlake build Formalization.FiniteQubitPauliSelection Formalization.CoherentPhaseQubitState Formalization\n   246\tlake build Formalization.CStarEquivExtension Formalization\n   247\tlake build AristotleCandidates.CStarInductiveLimitFeasibility AristotleCandidates.FiniteCoordinateMapNormControl\n   248\tlake env lean /tmp/stabilizer_hyperfinite_axioms_run4.lean\n   249\tpdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error .../001-tensor-product-trace.tex\n   250\t```\n   251\t\n   252\tLean reports only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound` for the\n   253\tchecked tensor-amplification, concrete dyadic direct-limit trace,\n   254\tfinite-support quadratic-phase, induced-trace, finite-qubit Pauli-word,\n   255\tpure/mixed product-state, basis-level coherence, phase-corrected direct-limit\n   256\tequivalence/state transport, and properly-infinite trace-obstruction theorems.\n   257\tNo source theorem contains `sorry`, `admit`, or a user-declared axiom.  The\n   258\tblueprint build output is kept under `/tmp`, not in this source project.\n--- formalization audit decision ---\n     1\t# Formalization semantic-audit decision\n     2\t\n     3\tDate: 2026-08-07\n     4\t\n     5\tThe approved deliverable is a semantic audit/tutorial of the Lean\n     6\tformalization, rather than another proof package or a compilation report.\n     7\t\n     8\tThe audit will provide:\n     9\t\n    10\t1. a status summary separating proved mathematics, conditional interfaces,\n    11\t   supplied data, and unformalized claims;\n    12\t2. a dependency map from the manuscript's finite-coordinate argument through\n    13\t   the algebraic limit, concrete C-star model, GNS transport, bicommutant\n    14\t   transport, and product-factor boundary;\n    15\t3. a theorem ledger mapping each important natural-language proof sentence to\n    16\t   exact Lean declarations, hypotheses, intermediate lemmas, and semantic\n    17\t   verdicts; and\n    18\t4. a step-by-step Lean reading sequence with code excerpts and direct build or\n    19\t   `#check` commands.\n    20\t\n    21\tThe audit must keep compilation separate from mathematical validity and must\n    22\tidentify exact manuscript locators from `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`. In particular,\n    23\tit must not call the regional hyperfinite-factor theorem proved merely because\n    24\tconditional Lean interfaces compile.\n    25\t\n    26\tThe first pass will inventory the current modules and completed-package\n    27\treviews. Later passes will inspect the principal declarations and their proof\n    28\tchains in dependency order.\n--- semantic audit ---\n     1\t# Formalization semantic audit\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: first inventory in progress.\n     4\t\n     5\tThis document is the approved semantic/tutorial audit described in\n     6\t`FORMALIZATION-AUDIT-DECISION.md`. It is not itself a proof and does not\n     7\tchange the manuscript theorem status.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Audit verdict convention\n    10\t\n    11\t- **proved**: the stated Lean theorem follows from its explicit hypotheses and\n    12\t  compiles without placeholders;\n    13\t- **conditional**: the Lean implication is proved, but one or more hypotheses\n    14\t  are supplied structures or external analytic assumptions;\n    15\t- **infrastructure**: reusable Lean mathematics with no manuscript claim;\n    16\t- **unformalized**: a manuscript step for which no corresponding theorem is\n    17\t  currently present;\n    18\t- **not aligned**: the Lean conclusion is stronger, weaker, or materially\n    19\t  different from the natural-language sentence.\n    20\t\n    21\tCompilation, axiom reports, and mathematical alignment will be recorded\n    22\tseparately.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Source clauses\n    25\t\n    26\tThe manuscript-facing audit uses only the locators recorded in\n    27\t`SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`:\n    28\t\n    29\t- `main.tex:1671-1679`: local symplectic basis and finite-support condition;\n    30\t- `main.tex:1740-1752`: regional factor theorem statement;\n    31\t- `main.tex:1915-1920`: proposed adapted-basis star-isomorphism;\n    32\t- `main.tex:1927-1931`: pure/mixed product density state;\n    33\t- `main.tex:1945-1967`: GNS, tensor, and factor claims;\n    34\t- `main.tex:1970-2004`: quasi-equivalence and normal-folium claims.\n    35\t\n    36\t## Initial module inventory\n    37\t\n    38\tThe detailed theorem ledger and dependency graph are the next sections to be\n    39\tfilled after inspecting the principal declarations. The current theorem\n    40\tcontract itself still says:\n    41\t\n    42\t```lean\n    43\tdef regionalAlgebraTheoremStatus : String :=\n    44\t  \"conditional contract pending analytic model\"\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\tThat status is consistent with the source contract and is not to be upgraded\n    48\twithout discharging the missing finite-coordinate, representation, normality,\n    49\tand product-classification premises.\n    50\t\n    51\t## First-pass verdict\n    52\t\n    53\tThe current formalization is a sequence of conditional bridges, not yet a\n    54\tformal proof of the manuscript's regional factor theorem. The strongest\n    55\tcurrently checked route is:\n    56\t\n    57\t```text\n    58\tfinite adapted-coordinate data\n    59\t  -> finite StarAlgEquiv at each stage\n    60\t  -> coherent algebraic direct-limit StarAlgEquiv alpha\n    61\t  -> compatible algebraic state transport\n    62\t  -> supplied compatible operator representation\n    63\t  -> closed operator-range C*-algebra\n    64\t  -> supplied state-preserving C*-extension\n    65\t  -> GNS unitary and algebraic bicommutant transport\n    66\t  -> conditional product-factor/type classification\n    67\t```\n    68\t\n    69\tThe arrows are not all existence theorems. In particular, the source-specific\n    70\tfinite coordinate realization, a faithful representation of the algebraic\n    71\tlimit, the regional C*-extension, the analytic normality/WOT statements, and\n    72\tthe product-factor classification remain explicit premises or missing\n    73\tmathematics.\n    74\t\n    75\t## Status table for the reviewed overnight packages\n    76\t\n    77\t| Package | Principal Lean object | Semantic verdict | Main external premise |\n    78\t|---|---|---|---|\n    79\t| P37 | `algebraicLimitOperatorRep` | conditional theorem proved | compatible finite-stage operator representations |\n    80\t| P38 | `quasilocalCStarAlgebra` | conditional theorem proved | supplied compatible representation; stage injectivity for faithfulness |\n    81\t| P39 | `StatePreservingData`, `gnsUnitary` | conditional theorem proved | C*-completion, alpha extension, state identity and continuity |\n    82\t| P40 | `bicommutantStarEquiv`, folium transport | algebraic part proved; analytic part conditional | WOT/double-commutant and normal-functional characterizations |\n    83\t| P41 | `ProductGNSFactorization` | partial boundary only | supplied factorization/classification; infinite weight interface is incomplete |\n    84\t| P42--P46 | no reviewed candidate currently present | not yet audited | candidate/archive status must be established before use |\n    85\t\n    86\tAll five reviewed packages compile in the local project. Their closeout\n    87\treviews explicitly separate compilation from mathematical scope. P41 is\n    88\taccepted only as partial: its infinite-pure `TracialWeightCore` does not yet\n    89\tassert positivity, normality, faithfulness, or semifiniteness.\n    90\t\n    91\t## Dependency spine and one-to-one theorem anchors\n    92\t\n    93\t### 1. Finite coordinate map\n    94\t\n    95\tNatural-language sentence:\n    96\t\n    97\t> Given finite adapted source and target word bases, send each source word to a\n    98\t> phase-corrected target word.\n    99\t\n   100\tLean anchor:\n   101\t\n   102\t```lean\n   103\tFiniteAdaptedCoordinateStarData.starEquiv\n   104\tFiniteAdaptedCoordinateStarData.starEquiv_word\n   105\tFiniteAdaptedCoordinateStarData.starEquiv_correctedWord\n   106\t```\n   107\t\n   108\tSemantic status: the construction is proved from the fields of\n   109\t`FiniteAdaptedCoordinateStarData`, but the actual manuscript-specific finite\n   110\tdata are not constructed from `main.tex:1671-1679` or `main.tex:1915-1920`.\n   111\t\n   112\t### 2. Coherence of finite maps\n   113\t\n   114\tNatural-language sentence:\n   115\t\n   116\t> The finite coordinate maps commute with the inclusions of stages.\n   117\t\n   118\tLean anchor:\n   119\t\n   120\t```lean\n   121\tstarEquiv_connecting_of_pauliBasis\n   122\tDirectedFiniteCoordinateFamily.stageEquiv_connecting\n   123\t```\n   124\t\n   125\tSemantic status: proved when word, label, phase, and connecting-map\n   126\tcompatibility are supplied.\n   127\t\n   128\t### 3. Algebraic infinite map\n   129\t\n   130\tNatural-language sentence:\n   131\t\n   132\t> Compatible finite maps descend to a unique star-isomorphism of algebraic\n   133\t> direct limits.\n   134\t\n   135\tLean anchor:\n   136\t\n   137\t```lean\n   138\tDirectedFiniteCoordinateFamily.alpha\n   139\tDirectedFiniteCoordinateFamily.alpha_of\n   140\tDirectedFiniteCoordinateFamily.existsUnique_alpha\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tSemantic status: this is a genuine algebraic direct-limit theorem, conditional\n   144\ton the finite coherent family. It is not yet a C*-completion theorem.\n   145\t\n   146\t### 4. State transport\n   147\t\n   148\tNatural-language sentence:\n   149\t\n   150\t> Pull the target product state back along the algebraic alpha.\n   151\t\n   152\tLean anchor:\n   153\t\n   154\t```lean\n   155\tTargetStageStates.targetLimitState\n   156\tTargetStageStates.pulledBackState\n   157\tTargetStageStates.pulledBackState_eq_targetLimitState_alpha\n   158\tTargetStageStates.pulledBackState_isAlgebraicState\n   159\t```\n   160\t\n   161\tSemantic status: the state-level implication is proved from compatible target\n   162\tstage states. The infinite product density operator itself is not constructed\n   163\tas a von Neumann-algebraic state in this module.\n   164\t\n   165\t### 5. Algebraic operator representation\n   166\t\n   167\tNatural-language sentence:\n   168\t\n   169\t> Compatible finite-stage operator representations define a representation of\n   170\t> the quasi-local algebra.\n   171\t\n   172\tLean anchor:\n   173\t\n   174\t```lean\n   175\talgebraicLimitOperatorRep\n   176\talgebraicLimitOperatorRep_of\n   177\talgebraicLimitOperatorRep_stage_congr\n   178\talgebraicLimitOperatorRep_injective\n   179\t```\n   180\t\n   181\tSemantic status: proved conditionally. Injectivity requires stagewise\n   182\tinjectivity; no infinite tensor-product Hilbert space is constructed.\n   183\t\n   184\t### 6. Concrete C*-model\n   185\t\n   186\tNatural-language sentence:\n   187\t\n   188\t> Complete the quasi-local algebra in the operator norm.\n   189\t\n   190\tLean anchor:\n   191\t\n   192\t```lean\n   193\tOperatorRepresentation.rangeClosure\n   194\tOperatorRepresentation.embedding\n   195\tOperatorRepresentation.denseRange_embedding\n   196\t```\n   197\t\n   198\tand, for the direct-limit range,\n   199\t\n   200\t```lean\n   201\tquasilocalCStarAlgebra\n   202\tquasilocalEmbedding\n   203\tdenseRange_quasilocalEmbedding\n   204\t```\n   205\t\n   206\tSemantic status: the closure is genuinely constructed inside bounded\n   207\toperators, but only after an isometric operator representation is supplied.\n   208\tThis does not prove that the manuscript's algebra has such a representation.\n   209\t\n   210\t### 7. State-preserving GNS transport\n   211\t\n   212\tNatural-language sentence:\n   213\t\n   214\t> A state-preserving C*-isomorphism induces a canonical GNS unitary.\n   215\t\n   216\tLean anchor:\n   217\t\n   218\t```lean\n   219\tStatePreservingData.sourceState_eq_pullback\n   220\tStatePreservingData.gnsUnitary\n   221\tStatePreservingData.gnsUnitary_intertwines\n   222\tStatePreservingData.gnsConjStarAlgEquiv\n   223\t```\n   224\t\n   225\tSemantic status: proved from supplied C*-completions, state positivity/order\n   226\tdata, continuity, and an exact state identity. It is not a proof of the\n   227\tmanuscript's state identity.\n   228\t\n   229\t### 8. Bicommutant transport\n   230\t\n   231\tNatural-language sentence:\n   232\t\n   233\t> The GNS unitary identifies the represented von Neumann algebras.\n   234\t\n   235\tLean anchors:\n   236\t\n   237\t```lean\n   238\tStarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated\n   239\tStarSubalgebra.le_vonNeumannGenerated\n   240\tStarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated_le\n   241\tbicommutantStarEquiv_sourceRepVN\n   242\t```\n   243\t\n   244\tSemantic status: the algebraic bicommutant/conjugation statement is proved.\n   245\tThe identification with strong/weak operator closure and the normal-folium\n   246\tstep are conditional interfaces, not proved analytic theorems.\n   247\t\n   248\t### 9. Product-factor and trace conclusion\n   249\t\n   250\tNatural-language sentence:\n   251\t\n   252\t> The product GNS algebra is a type-I pure factor tensor a finite type-I or\n   253\t> hyperfinite type-II1 mixed factor, with the appropriate trace conclusion.\n   254\t\n   255\tLean anchors:\n   256\t\n   257\t```lean cddddddddssssssssssssssssssssssssssw1\n   258\tProductGNSFactorization.pureFactor_typeI_of_finite\n   259\tProductGNSFactorization.pureFactor_typeIInfinity_of_infinite\n   260\tProductGNSFactorization.mixedFactor_typeI_of_finite\n   261\tProductGNSFactorization.mixedFactor_hyperfiniteII1_of_infinite\n   262\tfinitePureSector_product_classification\n   263\tinfinitePureSector_product_classification\n   264\t```\n   265\t\n   266\tSemantic status: the type labels and tensor identification are supplied data.\n   267\tThe finite algebraic trace transport is proved. The infinite-pure package does\n   268\tnot yet provide a faithful normal semifinite weight, so the corrected\n   269\tinfinite-sector trace conclusion is not formalized.\n--- adversarial closeout protocol ---\n     1\t# Adversarial Review Protocol\n     2\t\n     3\tUse this before closing substantive Codex work. The goal is to find defects, not\n     4\tto summarize accomplishments.\n     5\t\n     6\t## Scope\n     7\t\n     8\t1. Identify the artifact types touched: `code`, `math`, `tex`, `computation`,\n     9\t   `source-backed`, `workflow`.\n    10\t2. Inspect the diff or new artifact against the user's request.\n    11\t3. Report findings by severity first. If there are no findings, say so and state\n    12\t   the remaining risk.\n    13\t\n    14\t## Universal Checks\n    15\t\n    16\t- Did the work answer the actual latest user request?\n    17\t- Did it modify unrelated files or broaden scope?\n    18\t- Did it overwrite, reformat, or revert pre-existing user changes?\n    19\t- Are generated files, caches, logs, plots, PDFs, or build artifacts handled\n    20\t  intentionally?\n    21\t- Are final claims stronger than the evidence supports?\n    22\t- Are verification commands actually relevant to the changed artifact?\n    23\t\n    24\t## Code Checks\n    25\t\n    26\t- Does the changed behavior match the stated interface and edge cases?\n    27\t- Are tests missing for a changed behavior, bug fix, parser, numerical routine,\n    28\t  CLI command, or user-facing workflow?\n    29\t- Are errors handled where inputs, files, network calls, or external tools can\n    30\t  fail?\n    31\t- Is there hidden global state, path sensitivity, nondeterminism, race risk, or\n    32\t  stale cache behavior?\n    33\t- Did dependency, config, or environment changes alter unrelated workflows?\n    34\t\n    35\t## Math Checks\n    36\t\n    37\t- Are all assumptions, domains, signs, conventions, normalizations, and boundary\n    38\t  conditions stated before use?\n    39\t- Are definitions used consistently throughout the derivation?\n    40\t- Is any implication actually reversible, or was a one-way statement used both\n    41\t  ways?\n    42\t- Are there division-by-zero, invertibility, regularity, convergence, branch-cut,\n    43\t  gauge, or boundary-term assumptions?\n    44\t- Do limiting cases, dimensions/units, symmetries, and known special cases agree?\n    45\t- Are symbolic substitutions justified rather than smuggling in the result?\n    46\t- Are claims labeled correctly as proof, derivation, check, conjecture, or\n    47\t  numerical evidence?\n    48\t\n    49\t## TeX And Source-Backed Checks\n    50\t\n    51\t- Does the document compile if compilation was expected?\n    52\t- Are labels, references, citations, bibliography entries, and figure paths valid?\n    53\t- Are source-backed claims tied to exact locators when required?\n    54\t- Is quoted or paraphrased material clearly separated from the agent's synthesis?\n    55\t- Does the exposition define terms before using them in load-bearing statements?\n    56\t\n    57\t## Computation Checks\n    58\t\n    59\t- Is the computation reproducible from a recorded command?\n    60\t- Are inputs, random seeds, versions, tolerances, and units documented when they\n    61\t  affect the result?\n    62\t- Are outputs interpreted correctly and not over-promoted beyond the computation?\n    63\t- Were independent sanity checks used where feasible: small cases, limiting\n    64\t  cases, dimensional analysis, symbolic identities, or alternate algorithms?\n    65\t\n    66\t## Closeout Template\n    67\t\n    68\tUse this structure internally, then surface the concise version to the user:\n    69\t\n    70\t```text\n    71\tFindings:\n    72\t- [severity] file/path:line - issue and consequence.\n    73\t\n    74\tChecks run:\n    75\t- command -> result.\n    76\t\n    77\tResidual risk:\n    78\t- what was not checked, or why the artifact is not a proof/complete validation.\n    79\t\n    80\tCleanup:\n    81\t- remaining dirty/untracked files and whether they are intentional.\n    82\t```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
